AWS Virtual Private Cloud (VPC) gives you the power to create isolated network environments in the cloud

1. 𝗩𝗣𝗖 (𝗩𝗶𝗿𝘁𝘂𝗮𝗹 𝗣𝗿𝗶𝘃𝗮𝘁𝗲 𝗖𝗹𝗼𝘂𝗱)
A virtual network container in AWS where you can define your IP range (CIDR block). Multiple VPCs can exist in your AWS account.

2. 𝗦𝘂𝗯𝗻𝗲𝘁𝘀
Subnets divide your VPC into smaller sections.
• 𝗣𝘂𝗯𝗹𝗶𝗰 𝗦𝘂𝗯𝗻𝗲𝘁: Access to the internet via an Internet Gateway.
• 𝗣𝗿𝗶𝘃𝗮𝘁𝗲 𝗦𝘂𝗯𝗻𝗲𝘁: No direct internet access. Access the internet through a NAT Gateway.

3. 𝗥𝗼𝘂𝘁𝗲 𝗧𝗮𝗯𝗹𝗲𝘀
Defines how traffic moves between resources. Subnets are associated with route tables to ensure traffic knows where to go (e.g., to the internet or other subnets).

4. 𝗜𝗻𝘁𝗲𝗿𝗻𝗲𝘁 𝗚𝗮𝘁𝗲𝘄𝗮𝘆 (𝗜𝗚𝗪)
Enables communication between resources in your VPC and the internet. Critical for public subnets.

5. 𝗡𝗔𝗧 𝗚𝗮𝘁𝗲𝘄𝗮𝘆
Allows instances in private subnets to access the internet (e.g., for software updates) while keeping them secure and not directly exposed.

6. 𝗦𝗲𝗰𝘂𝗿𝗶𝘁𝘆 𝗚𝗿𝗼𝘂𝗽𝘀
Act as virtual firewalls at the EC2 instance level, defining which traffic is allowed in/out. Stateful: If inbound traffic is allowed, outbound is automatically permitted.

7. 𝗡𝗲𝘁𝘄𝗼𝗿𝗸 𝗔𝗖𝗟𝘀 (𝗡𝗔𝗖𝗟𝘀)
A stateless firewall that operates at the subnet level. You can allow or deny traffic.

8. 𝗩𝗣𝗖 𝗣𝗲𝗲𝗿𝗶𝗻𝗴
Connects two VPCs, allowing them to communicate privately as if they're on the same network. This is great for multi-account or cross-region scenarios.

9. 𝗥𝗼𝘂𝘁𝗲 𝗧𝗮𝗯𝗹𝗲 𝗔𝘀𝘀𝗼𝗰𝗶𝗮𝘁𝗶𝗼𝗻 (𝗥𝗧 𝗔𝘀𝘀𝗼𝗰𝗶𝗮𝘁𝗶𝗼𝗻): The process of linking a route table to a subnet. This determines how traffic is routed for that subnet.
• Without it, the subnet uses the default main route table not ideal.

10. 𝗔𝗪𝗦 𝗣𝗿𝗶𝘃𝗮𝘁𝗲𝗟𝗶𝗻𝗸
allows you to privately access AWS services (like S3, DynamoDB) and other services hosted within VPCs, without using the internet. It uses private IPs, making communication more secure and efficient.

𝗪𝗵𝗮𝘁 𝗬𝗼𝘂 𝗖𝗮𝗻 𝗗𝗼 𝘄𝗶𝘁𝗵 𝗔𝗪𝗦 𝗩𝗣𝗖
• Launch EC2 instances in custom subnets
• Control traffic flow using routing, IGWs, and NAT gateways
• Use Security Groups and NACLs to control access
• Connect VPCs through VPC Peering

𝗕𝗲𝘀𝘁 𝗣𝗿𝗮𝗰𝘁𝗶𝗰𝗲𝘀
• Always use a custom VPC for production.
• Separate public/private resources for security.
• Use VPC Peering cautiously to avoid network complexity.
• Leverage Terraform to manage infrastructure as code.

𝗥𝗲𝗺𝗲𝗺𝗯𝗲𝗿 𝗞𝗲𝘆 𝗖𝗼𝗻𝗰𝗲𝗽𝘁𝘀
• VPC: Your isolated cloud network.
• Subnet: Smaller network within a VPC.
• Route Table: Manages traffic flow.
• Security Groups & NACLs: Firewalls at different levels.
